WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 290: Athelstan: The Battle of Brunanburh, the First King of England, and the Unification

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:18.640
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series celebrates the sovereign who truly created England, the Golden Warrior Statesman who united Saxons, Angles, and Danes into a single realm.

4
00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:25.120
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of King Athelstan.

5
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:32.640
[George] Born around 894, Athelstan was the eldest son of Edward the Elder and a noblewoman named Ekwin.

6
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:41.440
[George] According to the 12th-century chronicler William of Malmsbury, young Athelstan was the cherished favorite of his grandfather, King Alfred the Great.

7
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:56.960
[George] In an extraordinary ceremonial investiture, the dying Alfred placed a royal scarlet cloak over the young boy's shoulders, buckled a gem-encrusted belt around his waist, and handed him a ceremonial Saxon broadsword encased in a golden scabbard.

8
00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:11.760
[George] Recognizing the child's destined greatness, to protect him from court intrigues, Athelstan was sent to Gloucester to be raised in the royal court of Mercia by his Aunt Ethelflid, the formidable Lady of the Mercians, and her husband Ethelred.

9
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:24.400
[George] There, Athelstan grew into manhood steeped in Mercian traditions of battlefield leadership, witnessing his warrior aunt systematically fortify towns and liberate Danish-occupied burrs across the Midlands.

10
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:33.680
[George] The ceremonial investiture of young Athelstan by King Alfred at the royal court of Winchester was a deeply symbolic passing of the generational torch.

11
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:41.680
[George] As the dying warrior monarch laid his hands upon the golden-haired grandson whose military destiny would complete the unification of Britain.

12
00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] Growing up in the halls of Gloucester under the watchful eye of his Aunt Ethelfled, Athelstan received a rigorous education in both classical Latin grammar and brutal medieval swordplay, riding alongside Mercian household troops as they constructed the fortified bars of Chester, Stafford, and Warwick.

13
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:13.480
[George] This dual heritage, royal Saxon blood combined with deep Mercian upbringing, made Athelstan uniquely capable of bridging the ancient cultural rivalries that had divided the kingdoms of the Midlands and the South for centuries.

14
00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:21.160
[George] When King Edward the Elder died in 924, Wessex and Mercia were nearly torn apart by succession disputes.

15
00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:27.560
[George] But following the sudden death of his half-brother, Athelstan was chosen king by the magnates of both realms.

16
00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:45.800
[George] On September 4, 925, Athelstan was crowned atop the ancient coronation stone at Kingston upon Thames, where Archbishop Athelm of Canterbury anointed him with holy oil using an innovative liturgical coronation rite that remains the ancestor of all British coronations to this day.

17
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.120
[George] Athelstan wasted no time.

18
00:02:48.120 --> 00:03:00.760
[George] When the Viking King of Northumbria, Citric Keach, died in 927, Athelstan marched his army north, storming the Viking capital of Jorvik, York, and expelling Citric's heirs.

19
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:10.280
[George] For the first time in history, an Anglo-Saxon king held direct sovereign possession of York, bringing the entire kingdom of Northumbria under Southern rule.

20
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:21.880
[George] Athelstan's coronation atop the ancient sandstone block at Kingston upon Thames on the border of Wessex and Mercia was a magnificent display of liturgical theatre engineered by Archbishop Athelm.

21
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:34.440
[George] The king was clothed in priestly vestments, handed the royal orb and sceptre, and anointed with holy chrism, establishing the sacred covenant between sovereign and realm that defined the English monarchy.

22
00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:42.600
[George] His military campaign to seize York in 927 was executed with ruthless speed, marching his army through the Danelaw.

23
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:58.960
[George] Athelstan stormed the fortified Viking stronghold of Jorvik, levelled its defensive ramparts, and seized the immense Viking treasury, distributing silver bullion to churches and poor citizens across the north to win the loyalty of the suspicious Anglo Danish population.

24
00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:10.000
[George] On July 12, 927, at Emont near modern day Penrith in Cumbria, Athelstan convened one of the most momentous diplomatic summits in British history.

25
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.800
[George] There, King Constantine I I of Scotland, King Highwald Dda of D.

26
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:20.400
[George] Hubarth, King Owen of Strathclyde, and Ildred of Bamberg met the Saxon sovereign.

27
00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:28.960
[George] In solemn ceremonies, these kings bowed their heads, renounced idolatry, and swore oaths of fealty to Athelstan as their overlord.

28
00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:43.600
[George] In official charters drafted by his royal scriptorium, Athelstan adopted the proud, unprecedented title, Rex Anglorum, King of the English, and proclaimed himself Basilius and Imperator of the entire island of Britain.

29
00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:56.560
[George] Athelstan began minting silver pennies bearing the inscription Athelstan Rex Totius Britanniae, Athelstan, King of all Britain, transforming an ancient dream into legal and monetary reality.

30
00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.960
[George] The historic summit at Emont was an unprecedented diplomatic triumph.

31
00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:10.160
[George] Standing beside the rushing waters of the river Emont in the shadow of the Cumbrian Fells, King Constantine I I of Scotland, King Hywild Dda of D.

32
00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:24.080
[George] Hubarth, and King Owen of Strathclyde knelt before Athelstan, swearing sacred oaths on holy relics that they would maintain perpetual peace, suppress pagan worship, and acknowledge Athelstan as their supreme overlord.

33
00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:30.000
[George] In his official charters, written in an ornate, elevated Latin prose known as the Hermeneutic style.

34
00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:41.640
[George] Athelstan declared himself King of the English and ruler of all Britain, asserting an imperial authority over the entire island that surpassed anything achieved by his Saxon predecessors.

35
00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.520
[George] Athelstan was far more than a conqueror.

36
00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:51.880
[George] He was an enlightened administrative genius who revolutionized English law, charity, and international diplomacy.

37
00:05:51.880 --> 00:06:07.560
[George] He issued six comprehensive legal codes, cracking down on rampant cattle theft, regulating weights and measures, establishing royal mints in every major bar, and mandating that local sheriffs distribute grain and livestock to impoverished paupers.

38
00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:23.080
[George] His court at Winchester was an international intellectual hub, frequented by Frankish, Breton, Welsh, and Irish scholars, where Athelstan collected precious Christian relics, including a fragment of the true cross and the holy lance of Charlemagne.

39
00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:30.600
[George] Recognizing the power of strategic dynastic marriages, Athelstan arranged alliances for his half-sisters across Europe.

40
00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:45.000
[George] One married Hugh the Great, Duke of the Franks, another wed Otto I, the future Holy Roman Emperor, while a third married Louis, King of Arle, making Athelstan the most respected sovereign in 10th-century Christendom.

41
00:06:45.000 --> 00:07:03.080
[George] Athelstan's legislative enactments, issued from royal assemblies at Greatley, Exeter and Faversham, established England's first centralized monetary system, mandating that only coins minted with the king's official likeness and bearing standard silver weight could be used across the realm.

42
00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:06.840
[George] His foreign diplomacy was breathtakingly cosmopolitan.

43
00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:11.760
[George] European princes seeking sanctuary, including Louis IV of France and Alan I.

44
00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:26.480
[George] of Brittany, were sheltered and educated at Athelstan's court in Winchester, while foreign monarchs competed to send him extravagant gifts, including the sword of Constantine the Great and precious holy relics encased in rock crystal and gold.

45
00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:36.320
[George] Athelstan remained unmarried throughout his life, dedicating his physical and spiritual energies entirely to the preservation and governance of his unified realm.

46
00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:45.200
[George] Athelstan's soaring hegemony sparked deep resentment among the Celtic and Norse realms of the north, who feared total Saxon subjugation.

47
00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:59.920
[George] In 937, a colossal triple alliance was forged to crush the King of England, King Constantine I I of Scotland, King Owen of Strathclyde, and the terrifying Viking warlord Olaf Gutfritsen, King of Dublin.

48
00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:10.000
[George] Sailing from Ireland with a massive fleet of 600 dragonships, Olaf landed on the west coast, joining forces with the Scottish Highlanders and Welsh mountaineers.

49
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:14.880
[George] It was the largest and most dangerous invasion of England since the Great Heathen Army.

50
00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:31.520
[George] Sensing an existential threat to the very survival of the unified English realm, Athelstan took his time, deliberately gathering a disciplined, battle hardened army from Wessex, Mercia and the Danelaw, marching northward with his 18-year-old half brother Edmund.

51
00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:36.880
[George] The anti English Confederation of 937 was born of desperate panic.

52
00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:45.640
[George] King Constantine of Scotland and King Olaf of Dublin recognized that if Athelstan were allowed to consolidate his administrative grip on northern England.

53
00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:50.720
[George] Their independent kingdoms would eventually be swallowed whole by the Saxon Colossus.

54
00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:56.480
[George] Olaf Gutfritsen's amphibious invasion was an extraordinary feat of Viking logistics.

55
00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:10.120
[George] Sailing from the River Liffey in Ireland with hundreds of longships filled with seasoned Norse Gaelic warriors, Olaf linked up with the Scottish army and Strathclyde Britons, marching down the ancient Roman roads toward the heart of England.

56
00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:23.000
[George] Athelstan remained calm, refusing to be lured into a premature engagement, deliberately allowing the invaders to exhaust their supply lines while he mobilized the entire military levy of the English realm.

57
00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:29.160
[George] The two colossal armies collided at an unidentified battlefield known to history as Brunnenburg.

58
00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:38.280
[George] The battle was an apocalyptic, day-long slaughter, commemorated in the Anglo-Saxon chronicle in one of the supreme heroic poems of old English literature.

59
00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:49.800
[George] Here Athelstan King, of Earls the Lord, of Bayorns the Ringgiver, and his brother also, Edmund Atheling, lifelong glory in battle won with the edges of swords.

60
00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:55.960
[George] The Saxon shield wall held firm under waves of screaming Norse berserkers and Scottish spearmen.

61
00:09:55.960 --> 00:10:04.200
[George] As the sun traversed the heavens, Athelstan unleashed his Mercian cavalry and Saxon axes, cleaving through the enemy lines.

62
00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:10.920
[George] Five young Viking kings, seven earls of Dublin, and Constantine's own son were cut down on the field.

63
00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:26.600
[George] Olaf fled with a handful of survivors in battered longships across the stormy Irish sea, while Constantine retreated to his frozen northern highlands, leaving the field piled high with corpses for the dark raven, the grey wolf, and the toad to feast upon.

64
00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:39.400
[George] The clash at Brunnenburg was the bloodiest battle fought on British soil before the Battle of Hastings, a ferocious day-long clash of iron and flesh where the fate of England hung on the stability of the Saxon shield wall.

65
00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:56.640
[George] The Anglo-Saxon chronicle recorded the slaughter with stark, epic majesty, as Axes' shattered shields and lances impaled screaming warriors, Athelstan and his young brother Edmund fought in the front rank, their swords stained red with the blood of Viking earls and Scottish Jarls.

66
00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:59.760
[George] The destruction of the Allied army was absolute.

67
00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:11.360
[George] Five young Norse kings and seven earls were cut down, while Olaf and Constantine fled the field in utter ruin, leaving Athelstan as the undisputed, supreme master of all Britain.

68
00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:17.920
[George] The victory at Brunnenbar was the definitive military milestone that secured the permanent unification of England.

69
00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:25.360
[George] Athelstan returned in triumph to his capital, having proven that the English kingdom could defeat any foreign confederation.

70
00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:37.920
[George] Passing away two years later in October 939 at age 45, Athelstan chose not to be buried in the traditional royal dynastic vault at Winchester alongside his father and grandfather.

71
00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:45.520
[George] Instead, out of humble devotion to his beloved Saint Aldhelm, he was interred at the Abbey of Malmsbury in Wiltshire.

72
00:11:45.520 --> 00:12:00.720
[George] Modern historians celebrate King Athelstan as the true founding father of the English state, a noble warrior, a pious scholar, and an enlightened king who took the shattered fragments of Anglo-Saxon kingdoms and forged them forever into the nation of England.

73
00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:10.320
[George] Athelstan's decision to be buried at the Abbey of Malmsbury rather than the royal mausoleum at Winchester was a profound gesture of humility and personal gratitude to St.

74
00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:16.160
[George] Aldhelm, whose divine intercession Athelstan believed had secured his victory at Brunnenbar.

75
00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:25.200
[George] His magnificent stone tomb, bearing his carved recumbent effigy crowned in gold, remains a sacred site in Malmsbury Abbey to this day.

76
00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:34.200
[George] By destroying the last great Viking coalition and uniting Saxons, Angles, and Danes under a single legal, monetary, and political crown.

77
00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:44.920
[George] King Athelstan did not merely defend a kingdom, he created the nation of England, forging an enduring national identity that has lasted for over a thousand years.

78
00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:56.440
[Rebecca] King Athelstan's military victory at Brunnenbar and administrative brilliance permanently established the unified Kingdom of England.

79
00:12:56.440 --> 00:13:06.760
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with the legendary Norse sovereign Senat the Great, who united England, Denmark, and Norway into a mighty North Sea Empire.

80
00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:09.240
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

